+zsh:12> python3 tools/stamp_drift/analyze.py --help
usage: analyze.py [-h] [--analyze-only ANALYZE_ONLY] [--output-dir OUTPUT_DIR]
                  [--json-out JSON_OUT] [--md-out MD_OUT] [--config CONFIG]
                  [--header-clock {UNKNOWN,D435I_GLOBAL_TIME,D435I_HARDWARE_TIME,SYSTEM_TIME}]
                  [--global-time-enabled {true,false,unknown}]
                  [input]

Analyze D435i ROS header.stamp against rosbag receive timestamps. The two
timestamps are deliberately kept as separate clock-domain values. The fit is a
diagnostic affine relation, not a latency measurement or an online clock map.
CSV input is ``topic,header_stamp_ns,receive_ns``. SQLite input is a rosbag2
sqlite3 database and uses ``messages.timestamp`` as receive time.

positional arguments:
  input                 CSV or rosbag2 .db3

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --analyze-only ANALYZE_ONLY
                        directory of saved raw input and configuration
  --output-dir OUTPUT_DIR
  --json-out JSON_OUT
  --md-out MD_OUT
  --config CONFIG
  --header-clock {UNKNOWN,D435I_GLOBAL_TIME,D435I_HARDWARE_TIME,SYSTEM_TIME}
  --global-time-enabled {true,false,unknown}
                        independent saved option readback; header domain does
                        not imply this value
